(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-3-pd1-exam3-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"PD1 Exam 3 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form D","category":"Physical Diagnosis","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is the introitus?","choices":["The vaginal opening","The area between the introitus and the anus","The domed superior portion of the uterus","The external urethral opening"],"answer":0,"exp":"The introitus is the vaginal opening. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is the perineum?","choices":["The shallow fossa medial to the labia minora, just below the vaginal opening","The hair-covered fat pad overlying the pubic symphysis and anterior pelvic bone","The area located between the introitus and the anus, and between the medial surfaces of the thighs","The thin membrane partially covering the introitus in a virginal female before intercourse"],"answer":2,"exp":"The perineum is the area between the vaginal introitus and the anus, bounded medially by the thighs. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"During passive range of motion, what does the examiner do?","choices":["The examiner moves the patient's joint through its range","The patient moves the joint entirely on their own","The joint is struck to elicit a reflex","The joint is only observed, not moved"],"answer":0,"exp":"In passive range of motion the examiner passively moves the patient's joint to assess range of motion, comparing the end points to the active range. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which element belongs in the plan rather than the assessment?","choices":["Referrals","The final diagnosis reached after history, examination and testing","Chronic conditions that also require ongoing assessment or treatment","Concurrent diagnoses identified alongside the presenting problem"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The plan covers disposition, testing, treatment, referrals, patient education, and follow-up; the assessment is a simple list of diagnoses. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Vibratory sense is tested by placing a 128 Hertz tuning fork over which location?","choices":["A bony prominence such as a distal interphalangeal joint","The belly of a large muscle such as the biceps","The center of the palm away from any bone","A tendon such as the Achilles tendon"],"answer":0,"exp":"Vibratory sense is tested by placing a vibrating 128 Hertz tuning fork over a bony prominence, such as a distal interphalangeal joint of a finger or the joint of the first toe. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 82"},{"q":"Grade 4 muscle strength is best described as what?","choices":["No evidence of any muscle movement","Full muscle strength against complete applied resistance","Only a trace of muscle movement","Full range of motion against gravity and some resistance, but weak"],"answer":3,"exp":"Grade 4 muscle strength is full range of motion against gravity and some resistance, but weaker than normal. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 18"},{"q":"What nipple findings should be specifically inspected for, including characterizing any discharge?","choices":["Only the nipple's color and pigmentation, without assessment of discharge or texture","Only whether the nipple is everted or inverted, without noting scaling or discharge","Nipple assessment is not part of the standard breast inspection and is deferred to a specialist","Inversion, retraction, scaling, or ulceration, and characterization of any spontaneous discharge"],"answer":3,"exp":"These are the specific nipple assessment findings, including noting and characterizing any spontaneous discharge. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 43"},{"q":"A patient who appears drowsy, opens the eyes and looks at you, but falls asleep when not stimulated is described as what?","choices":["Alert","Lethargic","Stuporous","Comatose"],"answer":1,"exp":"A lethargic patient appears drowsy, opens the eyes and looks at the examiner, and responds to questions but then falls asleep. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"How does the sample note anchor the top of the patient's pain scale?","choices":["A 10 for her is childbirth","A 10 for her is a previous migraine","A 10 for her is a fractured limb","The note leaves the top of the scale undefined"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The note records that 10 out of 10 for this patient is childbirth, which is exactly the anchoring the pain mnemonics call for. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is the key difference between the conventional Pap smear and the ThinPrep Pap Test?","choices":["ThinPrep does not require a speculum exam at all, only a blind vaginal swab for collection of the sample","The way cells are placed on the slide, with ThinPrep minimizing cell overlap, blood, mucus, and inflammation obscuring cells","ThinPrep eliminates the need for a cervical brush or spatula, relying only on a single cotton swab for collection","ThinPrep is a less expensive alternative that uses cheaper slide-preparation chemicals than the smear"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cells are collected and reviewed the same way in both methods; ThinPrep differs in how cells are placed on the slide, reducing obscuring artifacts. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 36"},{"q":"How should the chief complaint be recorded?","choices":["As a working diagnosis chosen by the clinician on arrival","As a summary of the review of systems in clinical terms","In the patient's own words, together with a duration","As a list of the abnormal findings noted on examination"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture requires the patient's own words with duration, explaining why the patient sought care and how long the problem has been present. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve carries the motor (efferent) limb of the pupillary light reflex, constricting the pupil?","choices":["The oculomotor nerve (cranial nerve three)","The optic nerve (cranial nerve two)","The trochlear nerve (cranial nerve four)","The abducens nerve (cranial nerve six)"],"answer":0,"exp":"The oculomotor nerve, cranial nerve three, carries the parasympathetic motor limb of the pupillary light reflex and constricts the pupil. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"What does the letter E stand for in ChLORIDEPP?","choices":["Exacerbating factors","Evaluation performed at a previous visit","Extra symptoms","Emotional response to the pain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture expands E as extra symptoms, sitting between duration and the palliative and provocative pair. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"The glenohumeral joint of the shoulder is formed by which two structures?","choices":["The medial clavicle and the upper sternum","The lateral clavicle and the acromion","The head of the humerus and the glenoid fossa of the scapula","The radial head and the capitulum of the humerus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The glenohumeral joint is formed by the head of the humerus articulating with the glenoid fossa of the scapula. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 32"},{"q":"Besides the final diagnosis, what else belongs in the assessment?","choices":["Only conditions that were newly diagnosed at this visit","Other conditions requiring assessment or treatment, including chronic and concurrent diagnoses","Only conditions that will be treated pharmacologically","Only those conditions the patient mentioned when giving the chief complaint"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture includes chronic conditions and concurrent diagnoses alongside the final diagnosis. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"When is the mental status typically assessed during the examination?","choices":["During the initial interview","Only after the reflex testing","Only during the sensory exam","Only while assessing the gait"],"answer":0,"exp":"Mental status is typically obtained during the initial interview, while cranial nerves are assessed with the head and neck exam. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"When examining axillary lymph nodes with the patient's arms raised, what is being assessed about the pendulous breast?","choices":["The patient must lie supine for this step to let the breast fall flat","Nothing different is done regardless of breast size, shape, or degree of ptosis","The exam is done leading the breast forward if pendulous, for better access","The exam is skipped entirely in patients with pendulous breasts"],"answer":2,"exp":"Leading the breast forward when pendulous improves access to properly examine the axillary nodes. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 28"},{"q":"In what order does the lecture say an oral presentation should run?","choices":["The same order as written reports","Beginning with the assessment, so the listener knows the conclusion first","In the order the information was obtained during the encounter","Beginning with the physical examination findings"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture then lists that order, from chief complaint and history of present illness through to assessment and plan. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Why is a pen light kept available as pelvic exam equipment?","choices":["It is not part of the recommended equipment list","To use if the speculum does not have its own light adapter","To sterilize the speculum's metal surfaces before each use","To check the patient's pupillary reaction before starting the exam"],"answer":1,"exp":"A pen light is available specifically for cases where the speculum lacks a built-in light adapter. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 21"},{"q":"What are the two numbered diagnoses in the sample note's assessment?","choices":["Viral upper respiratory infection and retained nasal foreign body","Acute sinusitis and viral upper respiratory infection","Allergic rhinitis alone, listed as a single problem","Acute sinusitis first, then allergic rhinitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The assessment lists acute sinusitis as number one and allergic rhinitis as number two. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Knee extension by the quadriceps is primarily innervated by which nerve roots?","choices":["C6, C7, and C8 (radial nerve)","S1 and S2 (sciatic nerve)","C8 and T1 (ulnar nerve)","L2, L3, and L4 (femoral nerve)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Knee extension by the quadriceps is primarily innervated by the L2, L3, and L4 nerve roots through the femoral nerve. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 39"},{"q":"What follow-up instruction does the sample plan give?","choices":["Return in six months for a routine scheduled review","Return only if a fever above 39 degrees Celsius develops at home","Follow up if symptoms have not improved in five days, sooner if worse","No follow-up is required once the prescription has been collected"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The plan gives both the interval and the instruction to return sooner for anything new, worsening, or otherwise concerning. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Where are the lateral axillary nodes located?","choices":["High in the axilla, centrally","At the lower border of pectoralis major","Along the lateral border of the scapula","Along the upper humerus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lateral nodes are located along the upper humerus. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"How is the vaginal introitus enlarged prior to speculum insertion?","choices":["Inserting the speculum fully open from the very first moment","No manual preparation is needed before speculum insertion","Applying upward pressure with one finger on the anterior margin","Applying downward pressure with two fingers on the posterior margin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Downward pressure with two fingers on the posterior introital margin enlarges the opening prior to speculum insertion. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"What is a bursa?","choices":["A band that connects bone to bone","A dense collagen matrix overlying a bony surface","A pouch of synovial fluid that cushions movement over a joint","A fibrous cord attaching muscle to bone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Bursae are pouches of synovial fluid that cushion the movement of tendons and muscles over bone or other joint structures. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which axillary lymph node group is described as the most frequently palpable?","choices":["Lateral nodes","Infra- and supraclavicular nodes","Central nodes","Subscapular (posterior) nodes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Central nodes, located high in the axilla, are the most frequently palpable of the axillary node groups. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"Where is the ulnar nerve palpated at the elbow?","choices":["Anteriorly, over the bicipital groove of the arm","Posteriorly, between the olecranon process and the medial epicondyle","At the radial styloid process of the wrist","Over the lateral malleolus of the ankle joint"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ulnar nerve is palpated posteriorly at the elbow, in the groove between the olecranon process and the medial epicondyle. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 40"},{"q":"How does the lecture describe the relationship between the history and the physical examination?","choices":["The physical examination should be completed before any history is taken","The history alone is sufficient when the patient is a reliable source","They are taught separately but are intertwined, each guiding the other","They are documented as a single undivided narrative section"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states the history guides the physical examination while examination findings elicit further history questions. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A patient describes chest discomfort and the clinician replies, \"So the tightness comes on when you climb stairs and settles when you rest — have I got that right?\" Which communication technique is this?","choices":["Summarization, which also shows the clinician was listening carefully","Echoing, which repeats the patient's last words back to them","Validation, which legitimises the emotions the patient reports","Signposting, which flags a change of topic before it happens"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes summarization as restating what has been heard; it demonstrates careful listening and helps identify what is known and what is still missing. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which structures does the examiner palpate on the posterior hip?","choices":["The patella, tibial tuberosity, and knee joint line","The medial malleolus and lateral malleolus","The greater tubercle and the bicipital groove","The posterior superior iliac spine, sacroiliac joint, and ischial tuberosity"],"answer":3,"exp":"On the posterior hip the examiner palpates the posterior superior iliac spine, the sacroiliac joint, and the ischial tuberosity. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 53"},{"q":"A cartilaginous joint, such as between vertebral bodies, is described as what?","choices":["Slightly movable, with bones separated by fibrocartilaginous discs","Freely movable, with a synovial cavity","Completely immovable, with fused bone","Freely movable, with a true ball-and-socket shape"],"answer":0,"exp":"A cartilaginous joint is slightly movable, with bones separated by fibrocartilaginous discs whose nucleus pulposus cushions bony movement. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which term describes a patient who is unarousable, with eyes closed and no response even to painful stimuli?","choices":["Stupor","Lethargy","Obtundation","Coma"],"answer":3,"exp":"A comatose patient is unarousable with eyes closed and shows no response to painful stimuli. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is distinctive about the distal ends of the fallopian tubes?","choices":["They contain the squamocolumnar junction","They open directly into the peritoneal cavity via the cervix","They are fan-like structures called fimbriated ends","They are directly fused to the ovarian surface"],"answer":2,"exp":"The distal ends of the fallopian tubes are fan-like, fimbriated structures located at the upper lateral angles of the uterus. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 16"},{"q":"On the reflex grading scale, what does a grade of 2 plus indicate?","choices":["Average, normal reflex activity","A hyperactive reflex with clonus","A diminished, low-normal reflex","No reflex response at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"A reflex graded two plus is average, normal activity; one plus is diminished, three plus is brisker than average, and four plus is hyperactive with clonus. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which item does the lecture list under technical issues?","choices":["Difficulty following the progression of the physical examination through the note","Inconsistency","Describing a finding only as normal without any supporting detail","Confusion between whether the document is a history and physical or a progress note"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Technical issues cover missing components, terminology, word errors, and inconsistency. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which system integrates motor, vestibular, and sensory information to produce smooth, coordinated movement?","choices":["The spinothalamic system","The autonomic system","The corticobulbar system","The cerebellar system"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cerebellar system integrates information from the motor, vestibular, and sensory systems to facilitate smooth, rhythmic movements and steady posture. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 83"},{"q":"During the sitting inspection, what maneuvers help exaggerate abnormal findings like dimpling?","choices":["Lying supine with both arms relaxed at the sides","Raising hands overhead and pressing hard against the hips","Holding the breath deeply for a count of thirty seconds","Bending forward at the waist, but only for small breasts"],"answer":1,"exp":"These dynamic maneuvers during sitting inspection exaggerate dimpling or retraction that might not be visible at rest. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 28"},{"q":"How should the labia majora be manipulated during inspection?","choices":["Retracted only after the speculum has already been inserted","Left untouched, relying on visual inspection alone","Pressed firmly together to occlude the introitus","Gently retracted laterally to expose underlying structures"],"answer":3,"exp":"The labia majora are gently retracted laterally to expose the structures beneath during inspection. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"How does the sample note describe the thorax and lungs?","choices":["Symmetrical expansion, resonant to percussion, clear to auscultation","Dull to percussion at both bases with coarse crackles on inspiration","Asymmetrical chest expansion with visible accessory muscle use","Wheezing throughout all of the lung fields on expiration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The note also records no visible masses or deformities, no accessory muscle use, and no tenderness to palpation. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is the vaginal fornix?","choices":["The thin membrane that partially covers the vaginal introitus","The domed superior portion of the uterine body, above the tubal insertions","The intravaginal portion of the cervix that protrudes into the vagina","Pockets made by the folded vaginal wall surrounding the cervix"],"answer":3,"exp":"The fornices are pockets formed by the folded vaginal wall around the cervix. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"Why should a water-soluble lubricant, rather than a gel lubricant, be used for the speculum?","choices":["A gel lubricant will interfere with the Pap smear","Gel lubricants cause more patient discomfort","There is no difference; either lubricant type is acceptable","Gel lubricants are more expensive"],"answer":0,"exp":"Gel lubricants can interfere with Pap smear cytology results, so water-soluble lubricant is specifically used instead. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"When testing hip adduction and abduction, what should the examiner do to isolate the movement?","choices":["Stabilize the opposite hip","Have the patient stand on one leg","Fully flex both knees to the chest","Apply the reflex hammer to the tendon"],"answer":0,"exp":"During hip adduction and abduction, the examiner stabilizes the opposite hip to isolate the movement and prevent pelvic tilting. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 54"},{"q":"What is colostrum?","choices":["A small sebaceous gland elevation found scattered across the areolar surface","Thick yellow discharge from the nipple near the end of pregnancy","Age-related glandular atrophy replacing breast tissue with adipose fat","A normal congenital variant of nipple inversion present since birth"],"answer":1,"exp":"Colostrum is a thick yellow nipple discharge that appears near the end of pregnancy. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"Besides the axilla and internal mammary nodes, where else might inferior breast lymphatics drain?","choices":["There are no alternative drainage pathways beyond the axillary basin","Only the inguinal lymph nodes situated in the groin, far from the breast's true drainage basin","Subdiaphragmatic nodes, abdominal lymphatics, and hepatic lymphatic pathways","Only the contralateral breast tissue, never structures below the diaphragm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Inferior breast lymphatics may drain into subdiaphragmatic nodes, abdominal lymphatics, and hepatic lymphatic pathways. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"What does disposition refer to within the plan?","choices":["The patient's attitude towards the proposed treatment","The order in which the problems are listed","Whether care will be inpatient or outpatient","The clinician responsible for signing the note"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines disposition as inpatient or outpatient and lists it first among the plan's elements. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Where do the medial portions of the breast primarily drain lymphatically?","choices":["Inguinal lymph nodes in the groin region","Deep cervical lymph nodes in the neck","Internal mammary (parasternal) lymph nodes","Axillary lymph nodes exclusively"],"answer":2,"exp":"The medial breast portions drain into the internal mammary (parasternal) lymph nodes. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 128 Hertz tuning fork is used in the neuro exam to test what?","choices":["Vibratory sense","Visual acuity","Deep tendon reflexes","Sense of smell"],"answer":0,"exp":"A 128 Hertz tuning fork is used to test vibratory sense, whereas a 512 Hertz fork is used for the Rinne and Weber hearing tests. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 17"},{"q":"What breast changes occur during pregnancy?","choices":["No changes occur in the breast during pregnancy, since hormonal shifts do not affect glandular or vascular tissue","Enlargement from glandular hyperplasia and increased vascularity, increased nodularity, and nipples/areolae darken and become more prominent","Breast size decreases due to glandular atrophy, reduced vascularity, and thinning of the areolar skin, resembling aging changes","Only the nipple changes color and size; the areola and surrounding glandular tissue remain completely unaffected"],"answer":1,"exp":"Pregnancy causes breast enlargement (glandular hyperplasia, increased vascularity), increased nodularity, and darkening/enlargement of the nipples and areolae. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which details does the lecture require for each medication?","choices":["Dosage, route, frequency, indication, and compliance","Manufacturer, cost, pharmacy, and insurance coverage","Only the drug names, since doses change frequently anyway","Prescribing clinician, start date, and planned stop date"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture requires all five, and includes prescription, over-the-counter, and vitamins or supplements. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"The abbreviation PERRL, used with cranial nerve three, stands for what?","choices":["Painless extraocular rotation, right and left","Pupils equal, round, and reactive to light","Position, extension, resistance, and reflex level","Palpate each rib, then reassess the lungs"],"answer":1,"exp":"PERRL stands for pupils equal, round, and reactive to light, documenting the pupillary exam related to the oculomotor nerve. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"Normal wrist flexion, bringing the fingers toward the floor with palms down, is approximately what range?","choices":["About 75 to 90 degrees","About 20 to 25 degrees","About 5 to 10 degrees","About 130 degrees"],"answer":0,"exp":"Normal wrist flexion, bringing the fingers toward the floor with palms down, is approximately 75 to 90 degrees. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 45"},{"q":"What does the lecture recommend before a return visit, where records are available?","choices":["Ask the patient to repeat the entire history again from the very beginning","Review the records and identify information needing update or clarification","Complete the note in advance from the previous entry","Delegate the update to another member of the team"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture recommends reviewing records before visits whenever possible and identifying anything needing to be updated or clarified, while maintaining focus on the patient so they feel heard. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"How are the ovaries assessed during the bimanual exam?","choices":["Only the pelvic hand is used, pressing directly and firmly against the cervix, with no abdominal hand ever pressing to trap the ovary","Abdominal hand on the lower quadrant while the pelvic hand is in the corresponding fornix, pressing lightly and pushing adnexal structures between the hands","Both hands remain flat on the abdomen only, palpating the lower quadrants from outside without any vaginal finger or fornix insertion","Ovaries are always easily palpable in every patient regardless of body habitus, obesity, prior surgery, or bowel gas present"],"answer":1,"exp":"This is the correct technique — abdominal hand on the lower quadrant, pelvic hand in the corresponding fornix, to palpate adnexal structures. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 40"},{"q":"In the OPPQRST mnemonic, what does the lecture say the letter O covers?","choices":["When it started, what the patient was doing, any trauma, and whether it was gradual, sudden, or chronic","Whether the pain occurs only at night, only on waking, or at no predictable hour","The organ system the clinician believes is most likely to be responsible for it","How often the pain has recurred over the past year and how long each episode ran"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture expands onset with all of these prompts. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which of the following does the lecture list as belonging in the subjective section?","choices":["Vital signs recorded at the start of the visit","The results of any imaging ordered at the visit","Medications and allergies","The ranked list of potential diagnoses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The subjective section covers pertinent positives and negatives, medical and surgical history, family history, social history, review of systems, medications, and allergies. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which finger pads are used for breast palpation, and what pattern is employed?","choices":["Two hands pressing simultaneously from opposite sides in a symmetric back-and-forth pattern","The whole palm of the hand, moving in a loose, unsystematic circular pattern","Finger pads of the 2nd, 3rd, and 4th fingers, using a systematic vertical-strip pattern","The thumb alone, tracing small circles around the areola and nipple"],"answer":2,"exp":"Breast palpation uses the finger pads of the 2nd, 3rd, and 4th fingers in a systematic vertical-strip pattern. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 46"},{"q":"What does the sample note record on examination of the neck?","choices":["Tender anterior cervical lymph nodes with the trachea still midline","A deviated trachea with visible masses in the anterior neck","An enlarged thyroid gland without any associated lymphadenopathy","No masses, trachea midline, no cervical lymphadenopathy or thyromegaly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. This is the neck examination as the sample note documents it. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Compared with a history and physical, the progress note is:","choices":["Broader, because it must restate the entire presenting history","More focused","Identical in scope but written in a different section order","Longer, because it accumulates every previous day's entries"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes the progress note as a more focused document than a history and physical. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What does the lecture identify as the value of validation as a technique?","choices":["It confirms the accuracy of the facts the patient has given","It signals that the interview is moving to a new section","It invites the patient to keep expanding, using their own last words as the prompt","It helps the patient feel their emotions are legitimate and understandable"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That is the lecture's definition of validation. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"During palpation of a joint, the examiner assesses for what?","choices":["Visual curvature of the spine","Reflex responses to a hammer","Tenderness, warmth, and irregularities","The patient's active range of motion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Palpation uses the hands to feel the joints, muscles, and surrounding tissues for tenderness, warmth, or irregularities. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 13"}]};
